WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:08.148
Module four is about dynamic inputs: the sensors and data feeds that keep a twin in step with reality, and the standards that let systems share them.

2
00:00:09.048 --> 00:00:10.860
Sensors fall into two groups.

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.984
Point sensors, like loops, radar and cameras, are accurate but only see one place.

4
00:00:15.984 --> 00:00:22.796
Network sources, like probe GPS and bus locations, cover the whole city but only sample some of the vehicles.

5
00:00:23.696 --> 00:00:25.583
Every source has a blind spot.

6
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:26.841
Loops see one point.

7
00:00:26.841 --> 00:00:30.427
Cameras struggle with darkness, rain and hidden vehicles.

8
00:00:30.427 --> 00:00:33.258
Probe data is biased toward certain vehicles.

9
00:00:33.258 --> 00:00:35.901
Connected-vehicle messages are still rare.

10
00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:38.354
That is why twins fuse several sources.

11
00:00:39.254 --> 00:00:44.547
Judge every feed on four dimensions: completeness, accuracy, timeliness and consistency.

12
00:00:44.547 --> 00:00:51.584
A feed that is accurate but arrives five minutes late is useless for signal control, and perfectly fine for planning.

13
00:00:52.484 --> 00:00:56.667
Standards let systems share data without custom code.

14
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:01.087
GTFS-Realtime and SIRI carry live public transport data.

15
00:01:01.087 --> 00:01:05.191
DATEX II carries road traffic information in Europe.

16
00:01:05.191 --> 00:01:08.743
SAE J2735 defines connected-vehicle messages.

17
00:01:08.743 --> 00:01:11.900
NGSI-LD is used by smart city platforms.

18
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.985
The goal is fusion: combining sources so that each covers another's blind spot.

19
00:01:16.985 --> 00:01:22.442
A camera that sees the queue plus a loop that counts reliably gives a better picture than either alone.
